{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Here.</b> Here the Gaon [Saadia], of blessed memory, said: if one objects and says — after HaShem said 'you shall not go with them,' how could He say 'rise, go with them'? — the answer is that HaShem did not want him to go with the first men until messengers more distinguished than they should come. In my opinion there is no need for this; rather, its reasoning is like that of 'send for yourself men.' For HaShem said to Israel, 'go up, conquer,' but they did not believe; rather, they said, 'let us send men ahead of us.' Then Moses asked HaShem and He said to him, 'send for yourself men.' And after HaShem said to Balaam, 'you shall not curse the people,' what need did he have to say 'that I may know what HaShem will add'? Rather, he harbored an evil thought in his heart, and HaShem said to him, 'go with the men — only take heed not to speak anything but what I tell you.' And the proof for my interpretation: 'Elohim's anger burned because he was going.'"], :book :numbers, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:18:58.081272074Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}